  "result": "I'm not going to derive PPO here, because it's well beyond where you are now. It also wouldn't help your makerspace assistant much.\n\nThe short version is that PPO is an RL method. The model tries tool-calling attempts and gets a score for each, for example \"booked the right machine at the right time\". PPO then nudges the model toward higher-scoring attempts, but caps each nudge so one lucky run can't wreck what it already does well. Labs do train their agents this way. But that means changing the model's weights, which needs a model you can train, many scored attempts, and GPUs.\n\n**Why this works for your project:** your budget is small and you call a hosted model. The cheap fixes are a clear tool description, a precise booking-tool schema, and a test set of about 30 real student requests that you re-run after each change.\n\nWhat this leaves out is the derivation itself. It builds on policy gradients, and that needs probability and expectation first.\n\nYour mission also lists training as out of scope. Do you want me to write a lesson on that test-set loop? It's the version of \"training\" that fits your timeline.",
